nodecode-command-tray is a rewrite of aydnOktay's aydnOktay/hermes-command-tray:

  https://github.com/aydnOktay/hermes-command-tray
  commit f28afd1e21dd9ac18756a4ec8d48e8a0f6889ce2
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 aydnOktay (LICENSE)

Rewritten: what the plugin kept, this cell keeps as data. The Hermes plugin
recorded every `terminal` tool call in a post_tool_call hook — the command,
the exit code, the working directory, a 160-character redacted output preview,
one row per unique command, the last 40 — and answered two tools
(command_tray_list, command_tray_clear), a /commands slash entry and a
state.json file under the plugin-data directory. Here the seam is Nodecode's
own shell, the eval verb (sh ...): one piece of advice around
NODECODE.EVOLVED:SH records the same row (command, ok or fail with its exit
code, the directory, the last non-empty output line, run count, timestamp),
with the same GitHub, OpenAI-shaped, AWS and `key = value` redaction, the same
40-row default and the same two tool names, now also reachable as
(command-tray:runs) and (command-tray:clear) through eval. The state file
moves to command-tray/state.json under the Nodecode home, and the settings are
a `command-tray` config section (max_items, preview_chars, redact).

Left out: the Hermes Desktop pane (desktop/plugin.js) and its dashboard API
(plugin_api.py) — a right-hand pane, a status-bar chip and the
hermes:composer-insert event that pasted a command back into the composer.
Those are Hermes's own web and desktop surfaces and have no Nodecode
counterpart; the data they showed is here, as the list, /commands and the
manual on (help :command-tray). Also left out: the per-row `tool` field (this
cell watches the one shell verb), and the pane's insert/copy clicks, which
existed so a person could reuse a command without running it; nothing here runs
a command either, so that property survives without the button.
